as the son of a blacksmith you're more  used to making weapons than you are  wielding them the combat and Kingdom  Come deliverance is tricky to master but  now I've been in enough scraps during my  playthrough I can give you a few hints  on how you can prevent yourself from  seeing this dreaded screen  while you can get by by focusing on one  weapon in particular I have found it  much more useful to diversify my armory  long swords and short swords have better  sharp damage that is slashing and  piercing  these are best against unarmored or  lightly armored opponents who are  wearing something your blade can easily  pass through against bandits and Knights  wearing chain or plate mail they're much  less effective if you are stuck with a  sword though try aiming for an unarmed  at area like the wrists face or thighs  heavier weapons with less of an edge  like maces axes and more hammers do much  more blunt damage these can cave their  way through thick armor and helmets  using pure kinetic force rather than  relying on a sharp honed edge you may  have the fanciest blade in the land but  against the night in full play a cheap  heavy mace will serve you much better in  my experience a short sword and shield  is best for the early game to handling a  long sword works well for the late game  or when you're comfortable with the  mechanics that I'll go into soon with  maces and axes just being a great  addition to your inventory if the  situation requires you switch so before  you head into combat take a look around  if it's a giant in heaving plates of  metal armor coming at you maybe get that  war hammer of your horses satchel  [Music]  one of the more advanced techniques is  to use weapon combos each school of  weapon has a bunch of combos that you  can learn as you level up the skill  these are fantastic for extra burst of  damage and to get through an enemy  hiding behind their blocks one resource  to help you learn these risk free is to  make the most of the combat arena in  Rattay where Bernard can help you out  with some practice sparring it's going  to be a little awkward to jump into your  skill menu and look at the combo list in  the heat of combat so practice practice  practice once you've learned a few  combos you'll also notice that they  target specific parts of the body like  the long surge reeve under or wrist  strike can target the weak point of an  unarmed forearm or use furless carmaker  strike to an attack and armored enemies  open-face an important thing to bear in  mind and something that I didn't realize  with the first 10 hours of gameplay is  that a long sword must be wielded  two-handed in order to use his combos  axes short swords and maces can use all  of their combos with the shield  now this one seems obvious but try not  to stand in the same place for too long  if you keep moving you can keep your  opponent guessing and there'll be a few  moments where just a simple step  backwards will mean the Bandit coming to  your throat will whiff is swing it's  also worth keeping in mind as you're  moving around the field of combat is  that positioning matters for example if  your right side is close to a nearby  wall or obstacle you won't be able to  swing from that angle your sword can't  magically phase through any structure  nearby but the same goes for your enemy  too this is especially important to keep  note of when you're attacking in  structures keep your blades off the wall  another smaller situational detail but  one that I've been able to use to my  advantage is your positioning if there  are archers on the field if you put the  unfortunate chap you're exchanging blows  with in between you and the guy with a  bow mr. archer will hesitate and hold  his fire just be careful not to give him  an opening and be aware that when your  melee combat and falls you're a target  once more  [Music]  it's almost never a good idea to hold  down the blog button when bad guys are  whaling on you you'll lose stamina and  eventually your head in the early parts  of the game you'll be taught the perfect  block that's when the green shield  appears in a screen and you block in  time for the attack a perfect block  won't take any stamina allowing you to  save valuable energy - well in your foe  a perfect block also gives you the  opportunity to respond with a repost an  attack that the enemy can only block  with a perfect block of their own  learning the timing of this can take  some time but again your pal Bernard can  help you with that at the combat arena  now after you've got the basics you can  go back to Bernard and he can teach you  the master strike if you impress him  with your feats of warfare  the master strike is essentially a  straight upgrade to the perfect block if  you block an attack at the very start of  its animation then you'll have a free  unblockable automatic attack I'd  recommend getting this skill as early as  possible so if you haven't got it  already head back to Bernie at the combo  arena around 10:00 or 11:00 a.m. and  start throwing down once your pummel  striking bandits on the nose you'll be  glad you got this skill there are many  moments when you'll be outnumbered by  foes and while it might not be the most  Knightly weapon the bow can be your  friend if not a tricky friend to use of  all the weapons in the game the bow is  the hardest master but if you can manage  to get a few cheap shots in or disable  enemy ranges before they can roar on you  that can really even out a battle  especially when you're outnumbered or  like Bernard said in the early game  archery tutorial drop a bandit running  at you and then draw your sword on his  friend and while it might not sit well  with the Knights of chivalry don't let  those runners get away they don't always  keep running a few times I've been  fighting a camp only to be shot in the  back by an archer that I wounded and let  flee so if a bandit is heading for the  hills don't be afraid to plant one in  his back after all it's all fair in love  or more  [Music]  so those are five tips that'll hopefully  serve you well in the fights yet to come  as I said the fighting system is pretty  tricky so you may have some hints of  your own if so be sure to leave them in  the comments section below and for more  Kingdom Come deliverance coverage stay  tuned to games.com  [Music]  you
 

